                                        <h2 class="featurette-heading fw-normal lh-1">
                                            Symptoms and Transmission:
                                            <span class="text-body-secondary"></span>
                                        </h2>


                                        <p class="lead">
                                            Symptoms and Transmission:
                                        </p>

                                        <span>
                                            <cite>LLM generated text(Needs verification from experts) from Model(Google Gemini)</cite>
                                        </span>
                                        <br />
                                        <p class="card-header">Symptoms:</p>
                                        <p>
                                            <b>
                                                Clinical Manifestations and Disease Progression
                                            </b>
                                        </p>
                                        <br />

                                        <div class="card-group">

                                            <b>Incubation Period:</b>
                                            The incubation period for Mpox, which is the time from exposure to symptom onset, typically
                                            ranges from 1 to 21 days. More commonly, this period is observed to be between 6 to 13 days ,
                                            or roughly 1 to 2 weeks. Studies conducted during the 2022 multi-country Clade II outbreak
                                            indicated an average incubation period of 7-8 days. Shorter incubation periods, ranging from 2
                                            to 4 days, have also been noted, potentially attributable to direct viral inoculation through sexual
                                            transmission.
                                            <br />
                                        </div>

                                        <br />

                                        <div class="card-group">

                                            <b>Prodromal Symptoms</b>
                                            The illness often commences with a prodromal, feverish phase, typically lasting 1 to 3 days.
                                            Common systemic symptoms during this phase include fever, intense headache, swollen lymph
                                            nodes (lymphadenopathy), back pain, muscle aches (myalgias), and severe fatigue (malaise,
                                            exhaustion, asthenia). Lymphadenopathy is a characteristic feature of Mpox, with swelling often
                                            occurring in the neck (submandibular & cervical), armpits (axillary), or groin (inguinal), and can
                                            be unilateral or bilateral. Respiratory symptoms, such as sore throat, runny or stuffy nose, or
                                            cough, may also manifest.
                                            <br />
                                        </div>

                                        <div class="card-group">

                                            A significant observation from the 2022 outbreak is that systemic prodromal symptoms
                                            sometimes began after the rash appeared in up to half of the cases, or were entirely absent in
                                            others. This deviation from the traditional presentation, where prodromal symptoms typically
                                            precede the rash, complicates diagnosis and requires clinicians to maintain a high index of
                                            suspicion. Patients may present with seemingly mild or atypical symptoms, such as isolated
                                            genital lesions, without any prior systemic illness, making Mpox harder to distinguish from other
                                            sexually transmitted infections or common skin conditions. This underscores the necessity for
                                            increased clinician awareness and education regarding varied presentations , and a lower
                                            threshold for diagnostic testing, particularly in at-risk populations. It also highlights the
                                            importance of thorough physical examination beyond just visible rashes, as internal mucosal
                                            lesions can occur.
                                        </div>
                                        <br />


                                        <div class="card-group">
                                            <p>
                                                <b>Characteristic Rash and Progression</b>
                                                The most common and distinctive symptom of Mpox is a rash or sores that can initially resemble
                                                pimples or blisters. This rash typically emerges 1 to 4 days after the onset of flu-like symptoms;
                                                however, for some individuals, the rash may be the very first symptom observed.

                                            </p>
                                        </div>

                                        <br />
                                        <div class="card-group">
                                            <b>
                                                The lesions progress through distinct stages over a period of 2 to 4 weeks :
                                            </b>

                                            <ul class="list-group">
                                                <li>Macules: These are flat spots, typically appearing for 1-2 days.</li>
                                                <li>
                                                    Papules: Macules evolve into raised, firm, and often painful lesions, usually lasting 1-2
                                                    days.
                                                </li>
                                                <li>
                                                    Vesicles: These lesions then become raised and filled with clear fluid, typically for 1-2
                                                    days.
                                                </li>
                                                <li>
                                                    Pustules: Vesicles transform into pustules, which are filled with opaque fluid, sharply
                                                    raised, firm, and frequently exhibit umbilication (a central depression). This stage typically
                                                    lasts 5-7 days before crusting begins.
                                                </li>

                                                <li>
                                                    Scabs/Crusts: By the end of the second week, pustules have crusted and scabbed over.
                                                    These scabs will remain for approximately 7-14 days before eventually falling off.
                                                    The rash often concentrates on the face, palms of the hands, and soles of the feet. However,
                                                    lesions can also appear on or inside the mouth, genitals, anus, or be diffusely distributed across
                                                    the body. Lesions are frequently described as painful until they enter the healing phase, at which
                                                    point they become itchy. Sores located in the anus or urethra can cause significant discomfort
                                                    and make urination or defecation difficult. During the 2022 outbreak, it was observed that
                                                    lesions were sometimes localized with an atypical distribution, and could present at different
                                                    stages simultaneously on the same patient, although lesions within a specific area typically
                                                    evolved together.
                                                </li>
                                            </ul>
                                        </div>

                                        <br />
                                        <br />
                                        <p class="card-header">Transmission:</p>
                                        <p>
                                            <b>Primary Human-to-Human Transmission Pathways:</b>
                                            <br />
                                            <span>
                                                <cite>LLM generated text(Needs verification from experts) from Model(Google Gemini)</cite>
                                            </span>
                                            <br />
                                            The primary mode of Mpox spread is through close physical contact with an individual who has
                                            the infection. This encompasses direct contact with the characteristic rash, skin lesions, scabs,
                                            or bodily fluids such as fluid, pus, or blood from the lesions.
                                            A significant observation from the 2022 global outbreak, particularly in the United States,
                                            revealed that Mpox spread almost exclusively through oral, anal, and vaginal sex, and other
                                            intimate physical contact. This includes activities such as rimming, hugging, kissing, biting,
                                            cuddling, and massaging. This transmission pattern was predominantly observed within social
                                            networks of gay men, other men who have sex with men (MSM), and transgender individuals.
                                            Studies from Spain and Germany during the 2022 outbreak further corroborated the high
                                            prevalence of cases within the MSM population, with inguinal lymphadenopathy suggesting
                                            sexual transmission as a primary route. This strong epidemiological link to sexual and intimate
                                            contact represents a substantial shift in the primary mode of human-to-human transmission
                                            compared to historical patterns, where such specific sexual transmission was not as prominently
                                            documented. This change in transmission dynamics underscores the necessity for public health
                                            messaging and prevention strategies to specifically target these high-risk behaviors and
                                            communities, moving beyond general contact precautions to include safe sex practices and
                                            targeted vaccination campaigns.
                                            Transmission can also occur through exposure to bodily fluids, lesions on the skin or internal
                                            mucosal surfaces (such as in the mouth or throat), and respiratory particles. While some studies
                                            suggest the virus can be found in respiratory droplets, information from real-life outbreaks
                                            indicates that the likelihood of human-to-human respiratory transmission across rooms, such as
                                            in markets or classrooms, appears low. However, prolonged close face-to-face contact can
                                            facilitate transmission. This nuanced understanding means that while widespread airborne
                                            precautions may not be necessary for the general public, healthcare workers and caregivers in
                                            close proximity to infected individuals require appropriate personal protective equipment,
                                            including masks and gloves. It also suggests that isolation measures should consider the
                                            immediate personal space of an infected individual rather than solely focusing on large room
                                            ventilation.
                                            Indirect Contact via Contaminated Fomites
                                            The virus can spread indirectly through contact with objects, fabrics, and surfaces that have
                                            been contaminated by an infected person and have not been disinfected. This includes items
                                            such as clothing, bedding, towels, dishes, eating utensils, fetish gear, or sex toys. While
                                            possible, this mode of transmission is generally considered less common than direct physical
                                            contact.
                                            Vertical Transmission
                                            Mpox can be transmitted from a pregnant individual to the fetus via the placenta. Additionally, an
                                            infected parent can transmit the virus to a child during or after birth through skin-to-skin contact.
                                            Contagious Period
                                            An individual with Mpox can spread the virus to others from the time symptoms first appear until
                                            the rash has completely healed and a fresh, intact layer of skin has formed underneath. In some
                                            instances, individuals may be able to spread Mpox one to four days before the onset of
                                            symptoms, although the prevalence of this pre-symptomatic transmission is not yet fully
                                            understood.
                                            Uncertainties and Ongoing Research
                                            Current understanding of Mpox transmission still has areas of uncertainty. It is not definitively
                                            clear how often individuals who do not exhibit symptoms can spread the virus. Furthermore,
                                            ongoing research is focused on elucidating how Mpox virus can be spread through respiratory
                                            secretions and whether it can be transmitted through semen, vaginal fluids, urine, or feces.

                                        </p>

